  <h1>The Heliostat</h1>
  <div class="story-meta">2026-05-25</div>
 </div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>"It's just resonance," Arun said, when she showed him the plot. "You tickled the field. It rang. Don't look at me like that. That's literally what we built this thing to measure."</p>
<p>"Resonance would decay," Lian said. Her mouth felt dry. "This persisted for the full six hours. It adjusted when we adjusted. Look."</p>
<p>She showed him the mid-run parameter shift she had introduced on impulse, changing the spacing by three percent. The active region's oscillations lagged, then caught up, matching the new spacing within two cycles.</p>
<p>Arun's jaw tightened.</p>
<p>"That could still be…some kind of feedback artifact," he said, but his voice had lost its certainty.</p>
<p>"Could be," she agreed.</p>
<p>They stared at the data together, two primates in a tin can, orbiting a star too bright to look at directly.</p>
<p>"What are you going to do?" he asked at last.</p>
<p>She thought of Halpern's email. <em>If you are right, you will not be believed; if you are wrong, you will join a long and interesting list…</em></p>
<p>"Nothing," she said. "For now."</p>
<p>He looked relieved and disappointed at the same time.</p>
<p>Days later, alone again in the observation bay, Lian anchored herself at the rail and watched the Sun.</p>
<p>She thought of all the stories humans had told about it. Gods. Chariots. Eyes. Hearts. She thought of how mechanistic science had scraped those stories away, leaving hydrogen and helium and fusion, which were true, but not complete.</p>
<p>"If you are anything like a mind," she said quietly, "we are terrible neighbors."</p>
<p>There was no flare. No miracle. Just light, constant and indifferent.</p>
<p>And yet, in the data, the same active region that had echoed her pattern showed a minor oscillation, out of phase with every known driver. It was as if something had cleared its throat.</p>
<p>Lian laughed, the sound thin in the recycled air.</p>
<p>"All right," she said. "We'll catch up."</p>
<p>It was not a promise she could keep on anyone else's behalf. Governments would ignore her. Colleagues would roll their eyes. Philosophers would write essays. The Sun, if it was anything like the paper suggested, had already outlasted more stories than she could name. It would outlast this one, too.</p>
<p>But the next time a paper crossed her screen with a title like <em>Toward a Conscious Cosmos</em>, she did not wince. She read it. Carefully. As if the universe had started clearing its throat a long time ago, and this was just another small, halting syllable.</p>
<p>Far below, on the blue planet that still called itself central, a child looked up at the sky and shaded her eyes with one hand, feeling a warmth that had been, for her, nothing but background.</p>
<p>Somewhere between the boiling plasma and that lifted hand, between entangled photons and flares that bent around unlaunched rockets, something like a pattern continued to form.</p>
<p>Whether anyone would ever call it a mind was almost beside the point.</p>
<p>What mattered, Lian thought, watching the restless, radiant disk, was that they had finally begun to ask a different kind of question—not <em>what</em> the Sun was made of, or <em>how</em> it burned, but <em>who</em> it might already be.</p>
<p>And like most good questions, this one changed the shape of the answers long before it ever earned the right to be true.</p>
